:::






The logistic regression is what you get when you write a linear model on that last scale, where addition is allowed and no value is forbidden, and then translate back to probabilities through the inverse of the logit, the logistic curve of @fig-glm_links. Each mouse $i$ has a yes/no outcome $y_i$ that follows a Bernoulli distribution (a coin flip that lands "completed" with probability $p_i$), and:

$$
y_i \sim \text{Bernoulli}(p_i), \qquad \text{logit}(p_i) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_{1i} + \dots + \beta_k x_{ki}
$$ {#eq-logistic}

That is @eq-glm with a Bernoulli family and a logit link, nothing more. Same recipe as every GLM in this part: pick the family, pick the link, keep the linear predictor.

## The first model: does genotype matter?

I'll start with only `genotype` in the model, before day-1 time enters the picture, because it lets us learn to read the output on the simplest possible case. `brms` fits it with the `bernoulli()` family, and `glm()` is its frequentist counterpart with the `binomial()` family (for 0/1 data a Bernoulli *is* a binomial with a single trial).

Before fitting, one decision that I'm not going to leave to a default. [The introduction to GLMs](glm-intro.qmd#sec-glm-intro) warned that priors on link-scale coefficients need to be judged on the outcome scale, and this is where that warning starts to matter. Here's what two candidate priors imply for the chance of completing, before seeing any data. Each histogram shows probabilities obtained by pushing draws of the intercept through the inverse logit.






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
set.seed(2)
prior_draws <- rbind(
  data.frame(p = plogis(rnorm(10000, 0, 10)), prior = "Normal(0, 10)"),
  data.frame(p = plogis(rnorm(10000, 0, 1.5)), prior = "Normal(0, 1.5)")
)
prior_draws$prior <- factor(prior_draws$prior, levels = c("Normal(0, 10)", "Normal(0, 1.5)"))

ggplot(prior_draws, aes(x = p)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 30, fill = "#0072B2", color = "white") +
  facet_wrap(~prior) +
  labs(x = "Implied probability of completing", y = "Prior draws") +
  Plot_theme
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![What a prior on the intercept says about the probability of completing, before seeing data. A wide-looking prior (left) is strongly opinionated on the probability scale.](logistic-regression_files/figure-html/fig-logistic_prior_predictive-1.png){#fig-logistic_prior_predictive width=672}
:::
:::






A `Normal(0, 10)` looks humble and behaves like a zealot: it believes, before seeing a single mouse, that nearly every animal either always completes or never does. `Normal(0, 1.5)` spreads its belief over the whole range of probabilities and still leaves room for the extremes. The logic is the one from @sec-priors-regularization: on a logit scale, log-odds beyond ±3 to 5 already mean probabilities above 95% to 99%, so a prior scale near 1 to 2 covers the plausible territory [@gelman2026; @inchausti2023]. I'll use `Normal(0, 1.5)` for both the intercept and the coefficient, and I'll refit with a much narrower and a much wider prior afterward, to show whether the conclusion depends on it. With only 12 mice that failed, I expect it to.






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(brms)

prior_logit <- c(
  prior(normal(0, 1.5), class = "Intercept"),
  prior(normal(0, 1.5), class = "b")
)

fit_group <- brm(
  completed_all ~ genotype,
  data = wire,
  family = bernoulli(),
  prior = prior_logit,
  seed = 123,
  file = "models/logistic-regression_fit1.rds",
  file_refit = "never"
)
```
