This is not a clean bill of health, and I'd rather tell you than hide it. The estimate is negative under all three priors, but it runs from -0.34 to -1.16, a threefold spread that depends on the prior more than I'd like, and the interval includes zero under every one of them. With 63 mice and 12 that didn't complete, the data alone don't settle how large the KO effect is, and the prior is doing real work, shrinking an estimate that is based on very few failures. That is a legitimate result, and it's what small experiments look like. It is also the reason to look at what else is going on in the experiment, which is what day-1 time is about to show us.

## Adding day-1 time: when the effect stops being one number

The dot plot at the start suggested that day-1 time matters, and that the KO mice are slower. Both are reasons to put `wire1_c` in the model: the mean time on the wire on day 1, in tens of seconds and centered at 25 seconds, so the intercept describes a wild-type mouse with a typical day-1 time and a coefficient is the change for ten extra seconds. Ten seconds is a little under a standard deviation of this variable, so the same prior scale we've used (`Normal(0, 1.5)`) is still a sensible, weakly informative one.






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
fit_time <- brm(
  completed_all ~ genotype + wire1_c,
  data = wire,
  family = bernoulli(),
  prior = prior_logit,
  seed = 123,
  file = "models/logistic-regression_fit4.rds",
  file_refit = "never"
)
```
