So does this family have the right idea about noise? Same question, same suspicion. The binomial says the scatter of a proportion around its mean is fixed by how many cells were counted and by the proportion itself, with nothing left over for animals that simply differ. To check, I'll ask the model where it expects each animal's proportion to land, and see whether the observed proportions honor that.






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
yrep <- posterior_predict(fit_binom, ndraws = 1000)
prop_rep <- sweep(yrep, 2, cell_prop$Pdgfrb_Total, "/")

check <- cell_prop[, c("DPI_f", "Pdgfrb_Pos", "Pdgfrb_Total")]
check$obs <- check$Pdgfrb_Pos / check$Pdgfrb_Total
check$lo <- apply(prop_rep, 2, quantile, probs = 0.025)
check$hi <- apply(prop_rep, 2, quantile, probs = 0.975)
check$outside <- check$obs < check$lo | check$obs > check$hi
check <- check[order(check$DPI_f, check$obs), ]
check$idx <- seq_len(nrow(check))

ggplot(check, aes(x = idx)) +
  geom_linerange(aes(ymin = lo, ymax = hi), color = "grey70", linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_point(aes(y = obs, color = outside, shape = outside), size = 2.2) +
  facet_grid(~DPI_f, scales = "free_x", space = "free_x",
             labeller = labeller(DPI_f = function(x) paste("DPI", x))) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("FALSE" = "#0072B2", "TRUE" = "#D55E00"), guide = "none") +
  scale_shape_manual(values = c("FALSE" = 16, "TRUE" = 17), guide = "none") +
  labs(x = "Animals, grouped by day", y = "Proportion PDGFR-β+") +
  Plot_theme +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_blank(), axis.ticks.x = element_blank())
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Observed proportion of PDGFR-$\beta$+ cells per animal (points) against the binomial model's 95% predictive interval for that animal (grey bars), 48 animals. Animals outside their interval are marked with a triangle.](logistic-regression_files/figure-html/fig-logistic_binom_check-1.png){#fig-logistic_binom_check width=768}
:::
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
dispersion <- function(m) sum(residuals(m, type = "pearson")^2) / df.residual(m)

disp_binom <- dispersion(glm_binom)
share_outside <- mean(check$outside)

glm_binom_all <- glm(cbind(Pdgfrb_Pos, Pdgfrb_Total - Pdgfrb_Pos) ~ DPI_f,
                     family = binomial(), data = cell_all)
```
:::






The bars are very narrow, because with hundreds or thousands of cells counted per animal, a binomial thinks a proportion should be pinned down almost exactly. The animals don't cooperate: 79% of them fall outside their 95% interval, where about 5% should. The standard summary is the [dispersion statistic]{style="color:green; font-weight:bold;"}, the sum of squared Pearson residuals divided by the residual degrees of freedom, which should be near 1 for a well-specified binomial [@inchausti2023; @gelman2020]. Here it is about 100. The binomial has no room for animal-to-animal differences in the underlying proportion, and there are plenty. The remedy is a change of family, to one that lets the proportion itself vary across animals, and it comes in @sec-beta-robust-regression, with the beta-binomial.

As for leaving Td79 out: putting it back changes the dispersion statistic from 100 to 103 and the day-3 log-odds from 1.21 to 1.32. The conclusion doesn't depend on it. I'd still rather not feed a model a number I know is wrong.

## When the logit breaks: separation

Every model so far worked because there were completers and non-completers (or positive and negative cells) in every group we compared. Here is what happens when that isn't true. The wire file has one such group: in `Klf4-Flox-Control`, none of the 7 mice failed a single crossing on any of the three days, which is one reason that group stayed out of this chapter. In `cell_data` there is a cleaner example, and it is real too. `Condition` is binary (sham surgery or MCAO), so we can ask a diagnostic question: **can we tell whether an animal had an ischemic stroke from its GFAP staining?** I'll code MCAO as 1 and standardize `Gfap_IntDen` (one unit is one standard deviation), so that a coefficient is the change in log-odds for a one-standard-deviation increase in staining, which is also the scale on which the priors recommended for logistic models are defined [@gelman2026].






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
cell_data$MCAO <- as.integer(cell_data$Condition == "MCAO")
cell_data$Gfap_z <- as.numeric(scale(cell_data$Gfap_IntDen))

sep_summary <- aggregate(Gfap_IntDen ~ Condition, data = cell_data, FUN = range)
```
:::






The problem is visible before any model: the largest `Gfap_IntDen` in any sham animal (97) is below the smallest in any MCAO animal (129). One cutoff separates the two groups perfectly, which is called [complete separation]{style="color:green; font-weight:bold;"}. It's a wonderful result for a diagnostic and a disaster for a maximum-likelihood fit: the likelihood keeps improving as the slope grows, so the maximum lies at infinity.






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
glm_sep <- suppressWarnings(
  glm(MCAO ~ Gfap_z, family = binomial(), data = cell_data)
)
sep_warnings <- tryCatch(
  { glm(MCAO ~ Gfap_z, family = binomial(), data = cell_data); "none" },
  warning = function(w) conditionMessage(w)
)
```
:::






`glm()` returns something, but it warns: "glm.fit: algorithm did not converge". The slope it reports is 367 per standard deviation, with a standard error of 73,581. Neither number means anything. The algorithm didn't converge to an answer; it stopped when the improvements became too small to see. A weakly informative prior turns this from a catastrophe into a statement. It doesn't tell the data what to say, it just refuses the absurd: coefficients that would imply probabilities of exactly 0 and 1. On a standardized predictor, `Normal(0, 2.5)` is the kind of prior the literature recommends for this: it considers a one-standard-deviation change in log-odds of ±5 (a jump from about 1% to about 99%) at the edge of what's plausible, and nothing more extreme [@gelman2026].






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
fit_sep <- brm(
  MCAO ~ Gfap_z,
  data = cell_data,
  family = bernoulli(),
  prior = c(prior(normal(0, 2.5), class = "Intercept"),
            prior(normal(0, 2.5), class = "b")),
  seed = 123,
  file = "models/logistic-regression_fit5.rds",
  file_refit = "never"
)
```
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
summary(fit_sep)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

